# Unit 5 Discussion: Search Algorithms

## Overview

This assignment compares linear search and binary search.

## Learning Objectives

* Implement linear search
* Implement binary search
* Compare performance
* Analyze algorithm efficiency

## Requirements

1. Test both algorithms on a small dataset.
2. Test both algorithms on a large dataset.
3. Demonstrate edge cases.
4. Analyze performance.
5. Create a real-world search scenario.



## Discussion Board Reflection

After completing the programming assignment, add this reflection to your initial discussion post in LEO.

Your reflection should be approximately 150–200 words and address the following questions:

1. What concepts or skills did you learn while completing this assignment?
2. What challenges did you encounter, and how did you overcome them?
3. Explain when to use linear versus binary search, including tradeoffs in real-world scenarios.



Reflection: 

Completing this assignment helped deepen my understanding of how algorithmic complexity translates into real performance. I learned that implementing binary search correctly solely relies on maintaining the constant and that the search window always contains the target if it exists. 



I would say, however, my biggest challenge was handling edge cases. A very prime example of this would be an empty list initially causing an index error in my first draft until I realized high = len(lst) - 1 becomes -1, making while low <= high safely skip the loop. It was definitely confusing at first and led to me also struggling to make the performance difference functional. But in the end, I solved these issues by using a 100,000-element dataset and searching for the worst-case last element. 



In real-world scenarios, I can see linear search being more appropriate for small, unsorted, or constantly changing collections, like checking a short list of logged-in users. This is mainly because sorting to enable binary search costs O(n log n) up front. Binary search, on the other hand, excels when data is large, sorted, and stable. The only thing that really comes to mind in this case would be looking up a word in a dictionary of some sort. All the while, the tradeoff is clear, with binary search demanding sorted data and random access and linear search working on basically anything but scales poorly as collections grow.

